  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nA beautiful fall weekend is in store for East Idaho as daytime \ntemperatures inch slightly warmer under the influence of high \npressure. By Sunday, afternoon highs will reach the 60s across \nmost lower elevations, ranging from right around 60 degrees in \nthe northern portions of the Snake Plain to near 70 degrees \nacross the Eastern Magic Valley and Raft River regions. \nOvernight lows will continue to drop into the 30s for most .\n\nEarly next week, the upper-level pattern begins to shift as a \nstrong low pressure system tracks south along the West Coast. \nAlthough deterministic guidance like the GFS shows the low \nswinging further offshore before dropping into the Desert \nSouthwest in a split-flow setup, ensemble means still track the \ncenter close enough to the coast to maintain light precipitation\nchances across our area through the day Tuesday. Main moisture \ntransport increases by mid-to-late week, with precipitation \nchances ramping up Wednesday night through Friday.\n\nConsiderable forecast uncertainty remains late in the week\nregarding total precipitation amounts and timing. Ensemble\nclusters continue to differ on the depth and structural\nevolution of the southern closed low as it begins to open up and\neject eastward. While the National Blend of Models (NBM)\nreflects a downward trend in QPF amounts heading into mid-week,\nwidespread precipitation chances remain 30% to 45% through\nWednesday, increasing to 50% to 70% Thursday into Friday.",
